  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nShortwave troughing working across the northern Rockies this\nafternoon is providing some lift to the monsoonal moisture in\nplace across the region. Convection thus far, has been richest\nacross the San Juans where a concentration of showers and\nthunderstorms have developed this morning. This activity is\nexpected to continue through the afternoon. Some rain is making\nits way to the surface, but rainfall remains sporadic. Some\nheavier showers could pose flash flooding issues this afternoon,\nas afternoon heating amplifies storms. Shortwave energy will\ncontinue to drag across the region into the evening hours,\nlikely keeping a few isolated showers going into the overnight.\nShowers will lose much of their stamina after sunset, so \nexpecting for more stratiform shower activity during the late \nevening to overnight hours, winding down before daybreak. While \nmoisture takes a slight downturn on Monday, there will still be \nample fuel for another afternoon of mountain showers and\nthunderstorms. Once again, some showers could pose a threat for\nflash flooding near burn scars and sensitive slot canyons,\narroyos, etc.\n\nAbove normal moisture sticks around Wednesday and beyond, as \nthe high gradually shifts back into the Desert Southwest. \nForcing will be somewhat lacking, with only the occasional \nshortwave rotating around the periphery of the high. This means \nthat most afternoons will see isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms develop over the higher terrain, with occasional \nperiods of focused convection coinciding with a passing wave. \nSo long as surface levels remain saturated, heavy rain and flash\nflooding will remain concerns, particularly for burn scars, slot\ncanyons, and flashy creeks/dry washes. Additionally, gusty \noutflow winds and lightning will be daily threats. Temperatures \nwill start around 5 degrees above normal, but will gradually \nwarm through the week under the influence of the high. By the \nend of the coming workweek, look for highs to be 5-10 degrees \nabove normal.",
